  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard to check how it renders. I also didn't test whether the rule fires, because a rule that only appears on the first scheduled run wasn't something I could check from here.\n\n**Data**: The `kibana_sample_data_logs` data view has `timestamp`, `bytes`, `clientip`, `response` (a string, with a `.keyword` subfield), `extension`, `geo.*`, `machine.os`, `request`, `url`, `agent` and `referer`.\n\n**Dashboard**: \"EFF-5ffa4650 Web logs overview\" (id `96b22d87-41df-40ee-84fe-76a166339e36`) has four panels and defaults to the last 7 days:\n- Total requests (count)\n- Average bytes\n- Unique clients (distinct `clientip`)\n- Requests over time (line chart)\n\n**Rule**: \"EFF-5ffa4650 5xx spike\" (id `dc233291-c6db-4e87-96b6-331b349470c1`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every minute over the last 5 minutes. It counts documents where `response.keyword` matches `5[0-9][0-9]` and fires when the count is above 10. It has no actions attached, so a firing only shows up in Kibana and doesn't notify anyone.\n\nMy first dashboard attempts were rejected by the API; I fixed the line chart's data source and the dashboard was created on the next try.",
